TITLE: Refresh large Dataset - Throttling Error
URL: https://www.reddit.com/r/PowerBI/comments/1f8uwte/refresh_large_dataset_throttling_error/
DATE: 2024-09-04T14:35:30
**subreddit: /r/PowerBI**
author: Fuxx7
### Refresh large Dataset - Throttling Error
Hi,

I am experiencing an issue with the refresh of a large Power BI dataset. While the refresh always completes successfully, the duration varies significantly, ranging from 30 minutes to 4 hours. This is due to multiple retries caused by throttling errors (as shown in the second screenshot). Although the refresh is eventually successful, I would prefer it consistently completes within 30 minutes.

We have already implemented several optimizations to improve the refresh time, including:


Implementing incremental refresh for the larger tables in the dataset.
Keeping the on-premises gateway consistently up to date.
Changing the StreamBeforeRequestCompletes setting from false to true in the configuration file, as explained here: Optimizing the On-Premises Data Gateway – The Data Evangelist (wordpress.com).
Upgrading the CPU and memory of the virtual machine where the on-premises gateway is installed.
Noticing that when we deleted all the historical partitions of the tables that we refresh incrementally, the refresh time consistently stayed below 30 minutes. However, retaining these partitions is necessary, so this is not a long-term solution.


Does anyone have any other suggestions or ideas we could try to further optimize the refresh process?

Thank you!

https://preview.redd.it/g4pylvq6ysmd1.png?width=828&format=png&auto=webp&s=7182794e9573db363d5184eaca604ab007020b48

https://preview.redd.it/6f6g9je8ysmd1.png?width=923&format=png&auto=webp&s=5e2abffc7170542dda9b3f718efe695948007407

https://preview.redd.it/ys3l3h3aysmd1.png?width=740&format=png&auto=webp&s=c10909f06d05f81da89f82b6b4d596d73ee26969

**votes: 3**
**comments: 20**

### Comments

-  **AutoModerator**
After your question has been solved /u/Fuxx7, please reply to the helpful user's comment with the phrase "Solution verified". This will not only award a point to the contributor for their assistance but also update the post's flair to "Solved". I am a bot, and this action was performed automatically. Please contact the moderators of this subreddit if you have any questions or concerns.

-  **Sad-Calligrapher-350**
I assume you have already removed all unused columns from the model (would improve overall refresh time). What capacity do you have?
Have you checked the capacity metrics for more details regarding the load on the capacity?
What about query folding?
What are your biggest, high cardinality tables, are those really needed (down the level of granularity that you have)?
